WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.706
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 8: Share Your Views.

2
00:00:03.786 --> 00:00:09.850
Unit 8 is about persuasion: how people share
a view and try to bring others with them.

3
00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:18.066
You read a school campaign poster, learn to separate a
fact that can be proved from an opinion that somebody holds,

4
00:00:18.146 --> 00:00:26.354
and take apart the persuasive toolkit of rhetorical questions,
emotive language, expert opinion and calls to action.

5
00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:32.783
Twenty-five words carry the unit, from evidence
and judicial to physiology and hemisphere.

6
00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:41.285
Six grammar lessons cover the present perfect with for and
since, adverbs of degree with comparatives and superlatives,

7
00:00:41.365 --> 00:00:45.857
adjective order, similes, and
sequencing words with modal verbs.

8
00:00:45.937 --> 00:00:51.716
In the story, Amal and Nora investigate
an ancient hall that stands without steps,

9
00:00:51.796 --> 00:00:55.859
and prove that a careful question beats a repeated legend.

10
00:00:55.939 --> 00:01:01.860
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

11
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:09.451
By the end of this unit, you will be able to read
persuasive posters, information texts and narrative,

12
00:01:09.531 --> 00:01:12.948
and identify how each one presents a viewpoint.

13
00:01:13.028 --> 00:01:20.017
You will also distinguish a fact that can be
proved from an opinion that somebody merely holds.

14
00:01:20.097 --> 00:01:28.128
You will also use adverbs of degree, comparative
adjectives and superlative adjectives to strengthen a claim.

15
00:01:28.208 --> 00:01:30.881
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

16
00:01:30.961 --> 00:01:38.066
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: intelligence,
citizenship, physician, geographer, ancient, magnificent.

17
00:01:38.146 --> 00:01:43.040
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

18
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.908
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

19
00:01:46.988 --> 00:01:50.268
The reading sequence includes Save Our School Garden!

20
00:01:50.348 --> 00:01:53.120
and Fact, Opinion and the Persuasive Toolkit.

21
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.007
Read actively.

22
00:01:54.087 --> 00:01:58.381
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

23
00:01:58.461 --> 00:02:02.501
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

24
00:02:02.581 --> 00:02:06.422
The first language focus is The Present Perfect Tense.

25
00:02:06.502 --> 00:02:10.851
The present perfect ties a past action to the present moment.

26
00:02:10.931 --> 00:02:14.626
We build it from have or has plus a past participle.

27
00:02:14.706 --> 00:02:20.144
Use it for experiences, achievements
and situations that are still going on.

28
00:02:20.224 --> 00:02:28.057
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

29
00:02:28.137 --> 00:02:32.641
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: One Minute to Persuade.

30
00:02:32.721 --> 00:02:37.294
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

31
00:02:37.374 --> 00:02:40.836
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

32
00:02:40.916 --> 00:02:44.517
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

33
00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:46.219
You are ready to begin.

34
00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:53.544
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

35
00:02:53.624 --> 00:02:57.095
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

36
00:02:57.175 --> 00:03:05.308
Use AI English for hints and feedback any time, and bring
your questions to a live session too if you join one.
